 ## Controllers
 
-The fifth tab in the lane's labels edits one MIDI controller over the whole clip — the sustain
-pedal, the mod wheel, any CC. The tab shows the controller's short name and a small arrow. Click it to
-show the lane; click it again to pick another controller:
+The **CC** tab shows a MIDI controller over the whole clip — the sustain pedal, mod wheel,
+or any CC. Use the dropdown inside the lane to select its controller. The dropdown shows
+the controller name and CC number, and offers:
 
 - the controllers the clip already holds data for come first, marked with an icon;
 - then **Sustain**, **Mod wheel**, **Pitch wheel**, **Channel pressure**, **Expression** and
   **Breath**;
 - **Other CC** lists every remaining controller number, in groups of 16.
 
-The first time you open the tab it shows the first controller the clip has data for, or Sustain
-if there is none. The lane names the controller in its top-left corner, and reads out the value
-under the pointer on the right.
+The first time you open the tab it shows the first controller with data, or Sustain if there
+is none. **+ lane** below the sidebar entries adds independent controller lanes. Each has
+its own dropdown and an adjacent **×** to hide it without deleting its MIDI. Closing the
+primary controller lane returns it to Velocity.
+
+The **127 / 64 / 0** scale on the left aligns with the graph; the pitch wheel uses a signed
+scale. The value under the pointer appears on the right. The controls sit above the graph,
+so clicking them does not draw automation. Extra lanes scroll vertically and share the notes'
+horizontal zoom and position. Drag a lane's upper divider to resize it. Heights return when
+you reopen the editor or restart Nota and re-add the corresponding lane and controller.
+With many overlapping clips, scroll their strip to reach every clip while keeping notes visible.
 
 **Pedals** — Sustain and the other on/off controllers (CC 64–69) — are drawn as blocks: the pedal
 is down wherever a block is.
